# The build context for infra/worker/Dockerfile. LANE N.
#
# THIS FILE'S ONLY JOB IS TO NOT EXCLUDE `scripts/`. The repository root
# `.dockerignore` line 51 excludes it -- correctly, for the five agent images,
# which install a package and never shell out to a script. The worker does exactly
# that: `agentorg/queue/runner.py:49` runs `python scripts/run_stage.py <stage>` as
# a subprocess per stage, and `scripts/worker.py` IS the container's entrypoint.
#
# Docker's rule, quoted from docs.docker.com/build/concepts/context:
#   "Place your ignore-file in the same directory as the Dockerfile, and prefix the
#    ignore-file with the name of the Dockerfile"
#   "A Dockerfile-specific ignore-file takes precedence over the .dockerignore file
#    at the root of the build context"
#
# So this file REPLACES the root one for this build -- it does not merge with it.
# Every exclusion the root file makes and this image also wants therefore has to be
# restated below. That duplication is unavoidable given the precedence rule, and it
# is why `infra/worker/Dockerfile` ALSO asserts `test -f scripts/worker.py` at build
# time: two ignore files that must agree is exactly the shape this repository
# distrusts, so the outcome is checked rather than the mechanism assumed.
#
# UNVERIFIED HERE. No Docker daemon runs on the machine this was written on
# (`docker ps` answers "dial unix /var/run/docker.sock: connect: no such file or
# directory"), so the precedence has not been executed. The build-time assertion is
# what makes that honest instead of hopeful.

# THE IMPORTANT ONE, copied from the root file's own note: build/lib/agentorg/
# holds a STALE copy of several modules from before the packaging fix, missing the
# agents/, common/ and security/ subpackages. Gitignored but present in a worktree,
# so a build context would carry it. Nothing may read it as truth.
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/

# Never ship a virtualenv. `.venv-*` here carry EDITABLE installs whose .pth files
# redirect `agentorg` imports back at a worktree that does not exist in a container
# -- and if it did, it would defeat the non-editable install this image is built to
# exercise.
.venv/
.venv-*/
venv/

# Secrets. `.env` holds a live GitHub token (gitignored). Excluded here as well so
# no build context can carry it into a layer, where it would persist even if a
# later stage deleted it.
.env
*.tfvars

# `runs/` is ~10k gitignored files and grows on every test run. CLAUDE.md says
# never to list it; shipping it into a layer is worse. Lane I measured what happens
# when a build traces this directory: Turbopack pulled the whole repository into a
# server bundle and three other gates could not see it.
runs/
runs-archive-*/

# Caches: stale bytecode has served wrong bytes in this repo before, and a host
# __pycache__ can shadow freshly installed source.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Not needed at runtime.
#
# `tests/` IS EXCLUDED AND THAT IS A DECISION, not a copy of the root file. The
# worker's stages import nothing from there. But note `scripts/preflight.py` DOES
# import `tests.provenance` -- so preflight cannot run inside this image, and it is
# not meant to: it is a pre-demo check run by an operator against the deployed
# account, not a container concern. If that ever changes, this line is what would
# have to change with it, and the failure would be a clean ImportError naming
# `tests.provenance` rather than a wrong answer.
tests/
target_repo/
docs/
tickets/
infra/Terraform/
infra/selfhost/
web/
.github/
.superpowers/
make_fixtures.py

# Git metadata: large, and it would put the whole history in a layer.
.git/
.gitignore
